            "english_quote": "before those who had assembled for him had found rest",
            "issue": "The associative dative αὐτῷ with συνεληλυθότας identifies people who had come with or joined Peter. “Assembled for him” instead suggests a gathering for his benefit or appearance and does not clearly preserve their companionship with him.",
            "suggested_revision": "Replace this wording with “before those who had come with him had found rest.” Submit the revised wording to fresh review.",
            "severity": "major"
          },
          {
            "source_quote": "οὐκ ἀπελείποντο δὲ οὐδὲ οἱ ἀποδεχόμενοι καὶ ξενίζοντες τῆς τῶν ἀξιούντων προθυμίας.",
            "english_quote": "Nor did those who received and hosted them fall short of the eagerness of those asking to host them.",
            "issue": "Understanding τῶν ἀξιούντων as prospective hosts requesting guests is contextually defensible, but the note omits the materially different reading “those requesting hospitality/lodging,” which could identify the accompanying travelers or those arranging lodging for them. The Greek does not expressly supply the object of the request. The noted alternative “those who considered it an honor” does not disclose this difference between requesting guests and requesting accommodation.",
            "suggested_revision": "Expand the note to preserve “those requesting hospitality/lodging” as an alternative. Explain that the subsequent disappointment of people without guests motivates the prospective-host interpretation, while the preceding arrangements for lodging support the alternative. The current main rendering need not be rejected solely because both readings are viable.",
